```java
import java.util.*;

// Expected the tags back in the order added, but used a HashSet
Set<String> tags = new HashSet<>();
tags.add("java");
tags.add("spring");
tags.add("sql");

System.out.println(tags);   // order unpredictable, e.g. [sql, java, spring]
// Also: TreeSet.add(null) would throw NullPointerException.
```
A `HashSet` makes no promise about order, so relying on it to remember insertion order is a quiet bug.

```java
import java.util.*;

// Insertion order required -> LinkedHashSet
Set<String> ordered = new LinkedHashSet<>();
ordered.add("java");
ordered.add("spring");
ordered.add("sql");
System.out.println(ordered);    // [java, spring, sql] — exact insertion order

// Sorted order + range queries -> TreeSet
TreeSet<String> sorted = new TreeSet<>(ordered);
System.out.println(sorted);     // [java, spring, sql] — sorted alphabetically
System.out.println(sorted.first());          // java
System.out.println(sorted.headSet("sql"));    // [java, spring]
```
Use `HashSet` only when order is irrelevant, `LinkedHashSet` for insertion order, and `TreeSet` for sorted order and ranges.

// Without equals/hashCode - allows duplicates!
class OrderBroken {
    Long id;
    String item;
    OrderBroken(Long id, String item) { this.id = id; this.item = item; }
}

// Proper implementation prevents duplicates
record Order(Long id, String item) {} // records auto-generate equals/hashCode

var brokenSet = new HashSet<OrderBroken>();
brokenSet.add(new OrderBroken(1L, "Laptop"));
brokenSet.add(new OrderBroken(1L, "Laptop")); // both added!
System.out.println(brokenSet.size()); // prints 2 - duplicates allowed

var properSet = new HashSet<Order>();
properSet.add(new Order(1L, "Laptop"));
boolean added = properSet.add(new Order(1L, "Laptop")); // returns false
System.out.println(properSet.size()); // prints 1 - duplicate rejected
System.out.println(added); // false - element already exists

var activeUsers = new HashSet<>(Set.of("alice", "bob", "charlie"));
var premiumUsers = new HashSet<>(Set.of("bob", "diana"));

// Union - combine both sets (mutates a copy, not activeUsers)
var unionSet = new HashSet<>(activeUsers);
unionSet.addAll(premiumUsers); // {alice, bob, charlie, diana}

// Intersection - only common elements (mutates copy)
var intersectionSet = new HashSet<>(activeUsers);
intersectionSet.retainAll(premiumUsers); // {bob}

// Difference - elements in first but not second (mutates copy)
var differenceSet = new HashSet<>(activeUsers);
differenceSet.removeAll(premiumUsers); // {alice, charlie}

// Immutable Stream approach for union
var unionStream = Stream.concat(
    activeUsers.stream(),
    premiumUsers.stream()
).collect(Collectors.toSet());

// Immutable intersection
var intersectionStream = activeUsers.stream()
    .filter(premiumUsers::contains)
    .collect(Collectors.toSet());

