import java.util.TreeSet;

// Stock prices kept sorted automatically
TreeSet<Integer> prices = new TreeSet<>();
prices.add(120); prices.add(75); prices.add(200); prices.add(95);

System.out.println(prices);            // [75, 95, 120, 200] — always sorted
System.out.println(prices.first());    // 75  (cheapest)
System.out.println(prices.last());     // 200 (most expensive)

// Nearest-element lookups (impossible in HashSet)
System.out.println(prices.floor(100));    // 95  -> largest value <= 100
System.out.println(prices.ceiling(100));  // 120 -> smallest value >= 100

// Range query: all prices in [90, 150)
System.out.println(prices.subSet(90, 150)); // [95, 120]
```

These ordered, range, and nearest-neighbour operations are exactly what justify `TreeSet`'s O(log n) cost over `HashSet`'s O(1).
